  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/4/4f/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_54.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Iron Man is out testing out his new face plate, when Pepper summons Stark back to his office.&nbsp; He's wanted at the Pentagon.&nbsp; When he gets there, they complain that his observer missiles in Viet Nam, which they have been taking pictures of behind enemy lines, have all either crashed or disappeared.&nbsp; Stark heads off to Viet Nam in person to take a look and begins to suspect the Mandarin's hand in this (first seen in [LINK:1963:TOS_50_iron0|TOS #50|2027-03-24]) - \"the most brilliant evil genius in the orient!\"&nbsp; Stark knows that the Mandarin has no love for the Reds either.&nbsp; \"His objective is to destroy <i>all</i> military might, so that <i>his</i> power will be supreme!\"\n\nHe decides to allow himself to be captured as Tony Stark, knowing that an all-out frontal assault by Iron Man would just draw out the Mandarin's defenses immediately.&nbsp; Then he gets into a Jeep and drives (somehow he knows <i>exactly</i> where the Mandarin's \"plush, heavily-guarded throne room\" is), and sure enough is soon captured by the guards.&nbsp; Their attempt to open his attache case causes them to activate the sleep gas booby trap, giving Stark time to change to Iron Man before Mandarin shows up in person to interrogate him.\n\nThe two engage, and when Iron Man throws off his various attacks, the Mandarin actually offers him to ally himself with him - \"Serve me - and together we can rule the world!&nbsp; For your power is second only to <i>mine</i>!\"&nbsp; (To which Iron Man thinks the exact same thing as I did when I was reading this - \"He <i>sounds</i> like a hold-over from the late late show\" - heh, it's as if Stan Lee realized how corny the offer sounded once he put the words down and had to immediately excuse it.)\n\nSoon the battle takes its toll on Iron Man's power levels - he needs a recharge fast!&nbsp; But he has no opportunity, and the Mandarin quickly snares him in magnetic steel cables that cling to his iron suit.&nbsp; He's trapped, knowing that if he had a full charge, he could snap them, but his power is almost completely depleted.\n\n\"The time has <i>come</i>, Iron Man!&nbsp; If you know any prayers, say them now, while you still can!\"\n\nTO BE CONTINUED!&nbsp; (in \"<i>Iron Man</i> #55,\" as the Next Issue blurb reads - note it's not even <i>Tales of Suspense</i> anymore)",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "the story is mostly a setup, with the mysterious threat of the disappearing missiles drawing Stark over to the \"orient\" to eventually face off against the evil Mandarin again.&nbsp; The actual mystery of where the observer missiles went is almost immediately solved, with Stark heading out to face the Mandarin, who he suspects is behind this.&nbsp; Stark's intuition of exactly where to find the Mandarin is uncanny, but I'll give him a pass here since he <i>did</i> once visit the Mandarin during his last encounter, so he more than anyone else might actually have known just where to go, but it did cause me to do a double-take at first reading!\n\nAs seems always the case with this villain, the Mandarin's rings serve as a plot crutch, where he can pull one power after another out of a hat to hurl at his enemy, and the combined effect serves to drain Iron Man's power down enough so that he is at last captured, now helpless, seemingly seconds away from death.&nbsp; And there we'll have to leave him until next month.&nbsp; But I wonder if there will be any further mention of the fate of the observer missiles...\n\nThe supporting characters were right where they always are - in Stark's office with Pepper busying doing her secretarial work while Happy just hangs around bothering her, and the two of them apparently doing nothing much else.&nbsp; It's hard to really begin to care what happens with these characters when they're not given much to do, so points off there...\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe story is certainly readable but not memorable.\n\n\nNotes (from fandom):\nIron Man first appears with his rivet-faced helmet in this story, replacing the horned helmet adopted in Tales of Suspense #48.\nThis comic features the first Iron Man story to not be a one-shot, as it is left to be concluded in the following issue.\nThis issue features Tony Stark smoking a pipe.\nIron Man last appeared in Avengers #6; He appears in next issue.&nbsp; <b></b><b> this is wrong!</b>\nThe Mandarin last appeared in Tales of Suspense #50; He appears in next issue.",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>Tales of the Watcher:</b>  \"Hands Off!\"\n(by Stan Lee, Larry Lieber and Chic Stone)\n\nThe story picks up after the origin tale from the previous month, where we learn why the Watcher's race must only passively observe, but it causes him great agony to watch, knowing that he could actually intervene to save this race or that planet.\n\nHowever, he eventually comes across a civilization that elects to shoot its atomic waste into space rather than let it infect their own planet, knowing that eventually, \"it will hit another celestial body... it will put an end to all life there!\"&nbsp; For decades, the Watcher checks back in on this floating time bomb until eventually, \"the cosmic space tides are forcing it toward a group of inhabited planets!\"&nbsp; If it lands on one of these, he knows it will destroy all life there.\n\nHe knows he has the power to stop it, but he is forbidden, and we see his anguish over the situation!&nbsp; \"If I do not act, millions will die!!&nbsp; And if I do act, I will be defying my most sacred oath!\"&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: As Ben Grimm might say:&nbsp; Wotta revoltin' development this is!\"]\n\nBut at that moment, a faraway super nova knocks one of this system's uninhabited planets out of its orbit right toward one of the inhabited ones, but wait!&nbsp; It strikes the radioactive atomic waste cloud, destroying both the runaway planet and the cloud.&nbsp; Holy cow, everyone is saved after all!&nbsp; Had the Watcher intervened and removed the cloud, the planet would have crashed into one of the inhabited worlds!&nbsp; \"Thus, by keeping my vow and doing nothing, I have saved an entire population!\"\n\nHis father was right after all!&nbsp; He must never intervene -- only look, listen and record!"
  ],
